% Two disjoint matched pairs on FOUR indices: a1 |><| b2 and a3 |><| b4, with (H1), (H2)
% on indices 1..4.  No vertex of the pattern {(1,2),(3,4)} has both an in- and an
% out-edge, so the walk argument (Theorem 4.8 / Prop. 4.10 of the notes) does not apply;
% nevertheless exhaustive search finds NO configuration with w != z among the projections
% of P_2, P_3, P_4, nor in the 57-element projection algebra of P_4/mu_1 (which violates
% the hexagon condition).  Question: does w = z follow from the projection-algebra axioms
% alone?  Run Prover9 with the goal below; for Mace4, move  cw != cz  into the assumptions
% (and delete the goal) to look for a projection algebra with w != z.
%   x * y = y x y  (= x theta_y).  Constants: a1..a4, b1..b4, cw, cz.
% (H1) is encoded by one equation per unordered pair, oriented so that every index occurs
% as the outer element (this is equivalent to full pairwise commutation with product cw:
% cw <= a_i for all i, and then a_i a_j a_i = (a_i a_j a_i)^2 = a_i (a_j a_i a_j) a_i = cw).

op(400, infix, "*").

formulas(assumptions).
x * x = x.
(y * x) * x = y * x.
(x * y) * x = y * x.
((z * x) * y) * x = z * (y * x).
(((z * x) * y) * x) * y = (z * x) * y.

% (H1)
a1 * a2 = cw.   a2 * a3 = cw.   a3 * a4 = cw.   a4 * a1 = cw.   a1 * a3 = cw.   a2 * a4 = cw.
b1 * b2 = cz.   b2 * b3 = cz.   b3 * b4 = cz.   b4 * b1 = cz.   b1 * b3 = cz.   b2 * b4 = cz.
% (H2)
a1 * b1 = b1 * a1.   a2 * b2 = b2 * a2.   a3 * b3 = b3 * a3.   a4 * b4 = b4 * a4.
% (H3)  a1 |><| b2,  a3 |><| b4
b2 * a1 = a1.   a1 * b2 = b2.
b4 * a3 = a3.   a3 * b4 = b4.
end_of_list.

formulas(goals).
cw = cz.
end_of_list.

% Weaker intermediate goals worth trying first (each on its own):
%   cz * cw = cw.        (w z w = w)
%   cw * cz = cz.        (z w z = z)
%   (b1 * b2) * a2 = a2 * b2.   (a2 z a2 = m2: an 'out-edge' relation at the in-only index 2)
